WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.840 --> 00:00:18.080
Libraries are centers of information. They are where we store the collective memories of the human species.

00:00:19.100 --> 00:00:25.500
And
therefore they protect the history of the Arctic

00:00:25.880 --> 00:00:31.760
after man started interacting
with the Arctic - first the idea and then

00:00:31.760 --> 00:00:36.500
the reality of it, and later the loss of
it.

00:00:36.740 --> 00:00:44.420
So I think libraries will be among the most important places we have

00:00:44.660 --> 00:00:52.960
in making people aware of what climate change means

00:00:53.320 --> 00:00:56.200
and that it is actually
happening.

00:00:56.460 --> 00:01:01.260
And the Arctic as a reference point

00:01:02.300 --> 00:01:13.180
is invaluable because that is
where we see it most clearly.

00:01:13.180 --> 00:01:19.360
That is the climate change and its effects on this
planet.

00:01:19.540 --> 00:01:22.300
And on human life because we

00:01:22.300 --> 00:01:31.400
should never forget that the edge
of the Arctic - the Arctic Circle - has been

00:01:31.400 --> 00:01:43.700
a home for human beings for millennia
and the new story is of course how the

00:01:43.700 --> 00:01:56.259
people who have lived there are losing
their homes, their hunting grounds and

00:01:56.400 --> 00:01:59.700
the place they call home.

00:01:59.700 --> 00:02:06.240
So I hope that
story is also in the archives

00:02:06.240 --> 00:02:11.580
because the fate of the people who live by the
Arctic

00:02:13.700 --> 00:02:17.340
is about the fate of humankind.

